---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { destinations, MONTH_SHORT } from '../../lib/data';

const sorted = [...destinations].sort((a, b) => b.lat - a.lat);
const fmt = (h: number) => (h >= 24 ? '24' : h <= 0 ? '0' : h.toFixed(1));
const tone = (h: number) => (h >= 16 ? 'great' : h >= 12.5 ? 'good' : h >= 9 ? 'fair' : 'poor');
---

<Guide slug="daylight-hours" photo="tromso">
  <p>
    Day length shapes a trip as much as temperature: a December day in London has under eight hours of daylight, while Tromsø
    gets none at all and Singapore has twelve hours all year. Long summer days mean more time for sightseeing and hiking; short
    winter days are perfect for northern lights. Here is the average daylight — sunrise to sunset — for every destination we
    cover, from north to south.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Near the equator</b> (Singapore, Bali, the Galápagos), days are about 12 hours long all year.</li>
    <li><b>In the mid-latitudes</b> (Europe, the US, Japan), summer days are 14–17 hours and winter days 8–10 hours.</li>
    <li><b>Above the Arctic Circle</b>, there’s a midnight sun in summer and polar night in winter.</li>
    <li><b>In the southern hemisphere</b>, the pattern is reversed: the longest days are in December.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Average hours of daylight by month</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table dl">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Average daylight hours by destination and month, north to south</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Destination</th>{MONTH_SHORT.map((m) => <th scope="col">{m}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          sorted.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={d.url}>{d.name}</a></th>
              {d.months.map((m) => <td><span class="h" data-rating={tone(m.daylight)}>{fmt(m.daylight)}</span></td>)}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">
    Hours from sunrise to sunset, averaged over each month and calculated from each destination’s latitude (including
    atmospheric refraction). Twilight adds usable light before sunrise and after sunset, especially at high latitudes.
  </p>
  <p>See also: <a href="/guides/midnight-sun/">midnight sun</a> and <a href="/guides/northern-lights/">northern lights</a>.</p>
</Guide>

<style>
  .dl th,
  .dl td {
    padding: 6px 4px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .dl th[scope='row'] {
    text-align: left;
  }
  .h {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 34px;
    padding: 2px 4px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .dl th[scope='row'] a {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
